perfect-forecast range - real miscalibration\"><circle class=\"chart-dot sig\" cx=\"262.21375\" cy=\"256.875\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><g class=\"pt\" data-tip=\"Forecast said 80%&amp;#10;It rained 69% of the time&amp;#10;Based on 68 days&amp;#10;outside the perfect-forecast range - real miscalibration\"><circle class=\"chart-dot sig\" cx=\"339.89852941176474\" cy=\"130.41176470588235\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><g class=\"pt\" data-tip=\"Forecast said 99%&amp;#10;It rained 79% of the time&amp;#10;Based on 202 days&amp;#10;outside the perfect-forecast range - real miscalibration\"><circle class=\"chart-dot sig\" cx=\"407.7239603960396\" cy=\"93.68316831683165\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"235.0\" y=\"422\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What the forecast promised</text><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"15\" y=\"200.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 15 200.0)\">How often it actually rained</text></svg>\n<figcaption>Green band = where a <b>flawless</b> forecast would still land, given\nhow few days we have. Red dots fall outside it, so those are real errors rather\nthan noise. The faint horizontal line is climatology\n(43%).</figcaption></figure>\n","season":"\n<h2 id=\"season\">Does the season matter?</h2>\n<p>In Jyvaeskylae the forecast is most skilful in\n<b>autumn</b> (0.50) and least in\n<b>spring</b> (-0.00).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th class=\"num\">Days</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Rain days</th><th class=\"num\">Skill score</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Avg error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>spring</td><td class='num'>127</td><td class='num'>32%</td><td class='num'>-0.003</td><td class='num'>17.7%</td></tr><tr><td>winter</td><td class='num'>175</td><td class='num'>42%</td><td class='num'>0.191</td><td class='num'>18.9%</td></tr><tr><td>summer</td><td class='num'>184</td><td class='num'>46%</td><td class='num'>0.261</td><td class